# Build OUTPUTS are not committed - but the scripts that produce them are. A bare `build/` rule stops git descending
# into the directory at all, so negations inside it never match, which is how build/sdk.sh (the Makefile calls it),
# build/link-null.sh and build/fetch-cuda-headers.sh (setup.sh calls both) came to be missing from every clone: the
# tree could not build itself and said so only at the first `make`.
build/*
!build/*.sh
!build/tinycc
# a rule with a slash in it is anchored here, so nested build directories need saying separately - the old bare
# `build/` had covered them by matching any directory of that name at any depth
libtinynv/build/
libtinynv/test/build/
*.o
*.a
*.dSYM/
cuda-13
*.fatbin
*.cu.cpp
spike/spike
spike/spike_real
libtinynv/third_party
spike/*.cubin
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# NOTE: the two rules above deliberately have no trailing slash. A worktree needs these paths symlinked in
# (they are large, fetched, and shared), and a `dir/` rule matches a directory but NOT a symlink standing
# where that directory would be - so the symlink is committable, and one nearly was, carrying an absolute
# path to one machine's checkout. Without the slash both the real directory and a symlink are ignored.
.DS_Store
